module bindings.rust.bind_mount_path_source_without_fd_anchor;

// mount(2) takes its source as a path, and the kernel resolves that name when
// the mount runs -- not when the caller last looked at it. A bind mount is the
// form where the source's content is the point: MS_BIND makes the mounted
// object be whatever the source path resolves to at mount time. The documented
// alternative is to hold the object rather than its name -- open the source
// once, stat and verify the file descriptor, and build the mount from the fd
// (open_tree with an empty path, move_mount onto an fd for the target). Code
// that mounts by name after validating the same name earlier has validated one
// resolution of the path and mounted another: between the two syscalls a
// planted link or an overmount redirects the source, and the mount then binds
// attacker-chosen content over the target. Container runtimes hit this shape
// wherever they mask paths by binding a fixed special file -- a device like
// /dev/null -- over entries of a root filesystem the container image supplied,
// because by then the process resolves paths inside the container's own mount
// namespace.
//
// What the binding recognises is the naming half in one function: a mount
// call whose flag argument carries MS_BIND -- nix's MsFlags::MS_BIND and the
// libc constant both spell it that way -- together with a path built from a
// string literal, the spelling of a source fixed by the program rather than
// chosen per call -- an absolute path literal, because a bind source is an
// absolute path and a per-call name arriving as a parameter is not this
// shape.
//
// Both needles are chosen around two measured constraints on a call-argument
// predicate, and neither is about the shape. A presence binding with no
// explicit requirement gates itself on its longest identifier-like literal:
// the program is skipped unless that literal occurs verbatim, and the
// frontend records a lone flag argument by its dotted name
// (call_arg:syscall.mount:MsFlags.MS_BIND, :: collapsed) while the source
// only ever spells MsFlags::MS_BIND -- a needle carrying the dotted form
// disables the binding for every file, which reads as a clean zero. The bare
// flag name occurs in the source under both spellings, so it is both matched
// and sound to gate on; an explicit `requires { content(...) }` is the other
// way past the gate and is not needed here. And a needle containing the
// literal's opening quote matches no argument atom at all, so the path needle
// stops at the slash and relies on the absolute-path prefix rather than the
// quoted literal.
//
// The negation is what turns "carries MS_BIND" into "is a plain bind". The
// two excluded flags are the documented forms that do not take a fresh
// source: MS_REMOUNT reconfigures a mount that already exists and ignores the
// source argument, and MS_REC applied to a bind turns it into a recursive
// bind of a subtree the caller already holds rather than a single named file.
// Everything else stays in: a plain bind of a hardcoded path over a target
// the caller does not fully control is the shape, whatever the source names.
//
// The judgement lives in the pack rule; this binding only recognises the
// atoms. Residual, false-negative direction only: a bind mount whose source
// path arrives as a parameter rather than a literal is not recognised, and a
// source anchored by an fd in a helper function leaves no atom in the
// mounting function to see either way -- the fd-anchored form produces no
// bare MS_BIND argument at all, so it falls outside this binding by
// construction rather than by a negation keyed on any helper's name.
binding bindMountPathSourceWithoutFdAnchor {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "rust" and node.context.call in ["mount"] and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["MS_BIND"]) and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["Path.new:/", "PathBuf.from:/"]) and not (containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["MS_REC", "MS_REMOUNT"]))
  emit issue code.BindMountPathSourceWithoutFdAnchor at node
}
